      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">1. Statutory Architecture: Public Notice No. 53 and Tokutei Katsudo</h2>
        <p>
          In March 2024, the Ministry of Justice (MOJ) and the Immigration Services Agency of Japan (ISA) promulgated Public Notice No. 53 and No. 54 under the <strong>Immigration Control and Refugee Recognition Act (出入国管理及び難民認定法)</strong>. This framework officially established the legal landing zone for digital nomads by inserting digital telework into the <em>Designated Activities</em> (特定活動, <em>Tokutei Katsudo</em>) category.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unlike work visas that require sponsorship by a domestic Japanese entity (such as <em>Engineer/Specialist in Humanities/International Services</em>), the digital nomad visa explicitly forbids entering into employment contracts with companies registered in Japan. The applicant must remain strictly engaged in international telework (telecommuting for overseas employers or providing independent services to clients based outside Japanese territory).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">2. The 10,000,000 JPY Annual Income Verification Protocol</h2>
        <p>
          The financial threshold is pegged at a rigid <strong>10,000,000 JPY</strong> per year. Because foreign exchange rates fluctuate against the yen, Japanese consulates evaluate income using official exchange conversion tables published by the Bank of Japan on the application date or historical averages over the preceding 12 months.
        </p>
        <p>
          To substantiate the 10M JPY threshold, caseworkers demand three cumulative layers of documentary evidence:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Layer 1: Historical Tax Returns:</strong> Official tax assessment forms from the applicant's country of fiscal residence (e.g., IRS Form 1040 / W-2 transcript for US citizens; P60 / SA302 for UK citizens; Notice of Assessment for Canadians) demonstrating historical earnings crossing the benchmark.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Layer 2: Active Employment or B2B Client Contracts:</strong> Contracts demonstrating guaranteed prospective remuneration for the duration of the planned stay, explicitly confirming permission for international remote telework.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Layer 3: 12-Month Banking Transaction Records:</strong> Consecutive bank statements showing regular recurring deposits that correlate with issued payroll or invoice settlements.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <!-- Income Conversion Benchmark Table -->
